  "query_preview": "..(Friday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 235 PM EDT WED OCT 21 2026\n\nAt the beginning of the long term period, an expansive and highly\namplified midlevel ridge will be located over the central CONUS; \nmeanwhile, deep troughing will be digging into the western CONUS, \nwith another trough over the East Coast, associated with a deep \nclosed low off the coast of Maine and another coastal impulse near\nthe Outer Banks. Although this troughing will shift over time, \nsuch an amplified ridge in the middle of the country, this setup \nwill lead to a very stagnant weather pattern locally.\n\nAt the surface, eastern Kentucky will find itself under the \ninfluence of persistent high pressure, which should keep weather \nquite uneventful through early next week. Each day, ample sunshine\nwill allow high temperatures to climb into the upper 60s to low \n70s, and each night, clear skies and light winds will allow for \nmoderate ridge/valley temperature splits to develop. MOS guidance \nsuggests that lows in the valleys will generally bottom out in the\nupper 30s to low 40s, and given such a dry air mass, this \nmagnitude of cooling seems reasonable. In fact, raw NBM dewpoints \nare likely too moist given relatively dry air just above the \nsurface, especially in the absence of appreciable moisture \nadvection. Thus, a drier dewpoint blend was favored, with \nresultant dewpoints in the low to mid 40s during the day, much \nmore in line with ensemble guidance. As usual, as our valleys cool\nefficiently overnight, fog formation is expected.\n\nBy early next week, an upper level low from the West Coast will \napproach the central CONUS ridge. As these features interact, the \nridge looks to flatten, and by Tuesday, flow aloft will be more \nsouthwesterly, which will work to increase mid and upper level \nmoisture. Meanwhile, at the surface, an area of low pressure will \nprogress through Canada, with a cold front draped across the Great\nLakes region. This front will attempt to sag south towards \neastern Kentucky, but with limited upper level support, it \ncurrently seems unlikely the front will reach our area by the end \nof the forecast period. Thus, the most consequential change in the\npattern will likely be increased cloud cover on Tuesday and \nWednesday.",
